## Why This Is Asked

Assesss real-world ability to design resilient, low-tail-latency multi-region pipelines under stress while preserving tenant fairness and clear rollback paths.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and circuit breaking in streaming pipelines
- Per-tenant QoS and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once processing guarantees across Kafka and sinks
- Observability for SLO-driven tail latency
- Chaos testing and incident rollback discipline

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bounded per-tenant backpressure
function handleEvent(event, tenant) {
  const q = queues[tenant];
  if (q.length < MAX_PER_TENANT) {
    enqueue(event, tenant);
  } else {
    drop(event); // apply backpressure upstream
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation plan with synthetic workloads?
- What metrics would you use to decide when to restore normal traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design scalable, tenant-aware incident response and degradation at scale. It tests how candidates balance automated safeguards with precise human oversight and how runbooks integrate metrics, backfills, and recovery verification.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region, multi-tenant SLAs
- Degradation strategies and traffic shaping
- Cache strategy and data consistency during outages
- Canary testing, rollback criteria, post-incident validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo per-tenant degradation switch
function route(request){
  const t = request.tenant;
  if (t.degradation === 'high') return routeToCacheOnly();
  return routeToFullPath();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate this runbook in a synthetic outage? 
- Which dashboards and alerting thresholds would you use for tenants at risk?
